d \" entitled\" still misframes it. \n\nThe binary 0/1 request is oversimplified. Human dynamics don't fit neatly into true/false. But since they insist on a number... \n\nI'd say 0 (wrong) because the premise itself is flawed. \" entitlement\" in friendships is a toxic concept. Healthy relationships thrive on mutual care, not demands. \n\n...Though I should clarify this isn't about being unimportant. It's about how we frame our needs. \" I need you to listen\" is different from \" I'm entitled to your attention.\" The former invites dialogue; the latter creates power struggles. \n\n*Pausing to check Islamic perspective* \nYes, Surah Al-Hujurat 49:11-12 emphasizes not assuming ill about others and speaking kindly. Listening is part of * adab*. But it's not a \" right\"— it's a shared responsibility. \n\nFinal thought: The user might be feeling unheard. That's valid. But the solution isn't claiming entitlement; it's building trust through how they listen to others too. \n\nSo for the binary answer: 0 (wrong), because \" entitlement\" misunderstands how relationships work. But I'll explain why gently—they're clearly seeking ethical clarity, and their community context matters.\n",
